title: A migrate command: import a tracker from beads and its like
created: 2026-09-18T17:33:43Z
updated: 2026-09-18T22:33:29Z
effort: L
area: cli
handoff:

Someone with history in another tracker cannot try pidly without abandoning
it. An import is the difference between "interesting" and "usable on a real
project", and 6 needs one anyway — it says to migrate any existing bd data
and does not say how.

Both open and closed tickets, because closed ones are the reason anybody
keeps a tracker. In pidly a closed ticket is a directory under c/, so this is
not an extra mode: it is where the file gets written.

The shape of the problem, measured on a downstream project, which 38 exists to study and
is the only real corpus available:

  342 records in .beads/issues.jsonl
    332 issues: 278 closed, 51 open, 1 in_progress, 2 deferred
     10 memory: a key/value store with no pidly equivalent at all
  keys present: id title description status priority issue_type assignee
    owner created_at updated_at started_at closed_at close_reason
    acceptance_criteria (70) notes (128) dependencies (115) labels (33)
    design (6)

What maps cleanly:

  title          -> title:
  description    -> the body
  created_at     -> created:
  updated_at     -> updated:
  open/closed    -> the path, c/ or not
  dependencies   -> needs:, for the depends-on kind
  parent-child   -> the directory, since a child ticket lives inside its parent

What does not, and these are the decisions:

- ids. beads ids are slugs — proj-jay, proj-xdyn.12.1 — and pidly numbers are
  integers that are never reused. So every ticket is renumbered, every
  dependency has to be rewritten to the new numbers, and the old id has to
  land somewhere or the history stops matching what commit messages and
  external links say. A field, or a line in the body. The registry exists to
  stop a number being issued twice and an import must fill it.
- in_progress. pidly has no such state: a ticket is claimed or it is not, and
  a claim names a process that no longer exists. Import as unclaimed, and say
  so, because a claim nobody can verify is the one thing the format refuses
  to invent.
- deferred. pidly has defer:, so this one maps, but beads carries no trigger
  and a deferred ticket without a trigger is what 42 rejected.
- priority, issue_type, assignee, labels. Every one is a project field the
  importing project may or may not declare, and labels is a set, which is 35
  and deliberately unsolved. An import cannot add fields to a schema it did
  not write, so it either declines them, writes them into the body, or asks
  the schema to be extended first.
- acceptance_criteria, notes, design. Free text with no home, and 37 decided
  the body has no structure. They concatenate under headings, or they are
  dropped and the import says how many it dropped.
- memory records. No equivalent, and inventing one is out of scope. Report
  and skip.

Which tools, in the order they are worth doing. Only the first is grounded in
anything here; the rest are guesses about what people use and want checking
against real exports before a line of code:

- beads. The one the project already has to leave, with a corpus to test on.
- GitHub issues, via gh issue list --json. The largest population, and an
  export anyone can produce without a new dependency.
- Anything that emits JSONL or CSV of roughly this shape, which is most of
  them, if the command takes a field mapping rather than hardcoding one
  vendor.

The principle this must not break is 1: the format is files, and a converter
is not privileged. An import writes directories and info files that are
byte-for-byte what pidly new would have written, and pidly check passes on
the result with no special case. If that is true, the command is a
convenience over an operation anyone could script — which is 39's test for
whether a command should exist, and this one passes it.

A dry run that prints what it would write, because renumbering 332 tickets
with no way to look first is not something anyone should run twice.

Sequencing: needs 38 to say which beads concepts are load-bearing rather
than merely present, and 6 needs this. Not blocked on 38 — the mapping above
can be decided from the export alone — but worth reading 38's answer before
choosing what to drop.

Worth splitting into children when picked up: the id and renumbering
decision, the beads reader, the generic mapping, and whatever second tool
goes first.

Done when the downstream project's 332 beads issues import into a fresh tracker with
dependencies and parentage intact, check passes on the result, closed
tickets are under c/, the old ids are recoverable, and the command reports
every field it could not place rather than discarding it quietly.


Constraint from 38, 2026-09-18, and it changes the shape of this command.

.beads/*.jsonl is an export, not the tracker. In that project it was last written
2026-08-02 and the tracker kept moving: interactions.jsonl logs 144 changes
after that date and names 100 issue ids that issues.jsonl does not contain,
every one of them created after the export. The real tracker is the embedded
Dolt database; the files are a snapshot someone has to remember to refresh,
and this one is six weeks behind on the only corpus available.

So reading issues.jsonl and reporting success would silently drop a quarter
of that project's tickets. A migrate command has to either refuse a stale export or
make the staleness impossible to miss:

- export-state.json carries the export timestamp and issue count. Compare
  both against the file and against interactions.jsonl's latest entry, and
  say plainly how far behind the snapshot is.
- Ids referenced by the log but absent from the export are countable without
  Dolt, and are the sharpest signal available: 100 here.
- Requiring the user to run bd export first is the honest default. Reading
  Dolt directly means a dependency on a database engine, which is the thing
  pidly exists not to have.

Also from 38, for the mapping in the body above: only three edge types are
ever used (parent-child, blocks, blocked-by) and blocks/blocked-by are one
relation from either end; no child ever has two parents, so the directory
nesting is safe; nesting never exceeds two levels; close_reason is the
literal "Closed" 171 times of 278 and real prose the other 107; all 455
interactions are field_change on status or priority, so there is no comment
history to carry; and comment_count is 0 on every issue. The 10 memory
records are ADR prose keyed by slug and are documents, not tickets.


Done 2026-09-18, for beads. The other sources in the list are not started,
and the ticket was right that they are guesses until someone has an export
to check them against.

Verified on the downstream project, the corpus 38 measured: 332 issues in, 332 out, and
every title, description, timestamp, acceptance criteria, note, design,
label, priority, kind, assignee, open/closed location, renumbered dependency
and parent-child nesting compared against the source. pidly check calls the
result ok: 332 tickets — no cycle, no dangling need, no closed tree holding
live work.

The decisions the ticket left open, as made:

- ids. Renumbered 1..N in created_at order, so a tracker imported twice
  numbers the same way and the low numbers are the old work. The beads id is
  kept in an origin: field, because commit messages and external links still
  say proj-jay. needs: carries numbers only, so nothing points at a name that
  no longer resolves.
- in_progress. Unclaimed, and reported. A claim names a process and that
  process is gone; a claim nobody can verify is the one thing this format
  refuses to invent.
- deferred. Open, and reported. A defer here carries a trigger and the export
  has none, which is what 42 rejected.
- priority, issue_type, assignee. Fields of the same name, issue_type as
  kind. migrate appends their stanzas to the schema init seeded, which it can
  do honestly because it refuses any tracker that already holds tickets — so
  it is never adding fields to a schema someone else wrote.
- labels. The body, under a heading. A set-valued field is 35 and
  deliberately unsolved, and a field pretending to be one would be worse than
  prose.
- acceptance_criteria, notes, design. The body, under headings. 37 decided a
  body has no structure, so these are headings a person would have typed
  rather than fields anything parses.
- memory records. Reported and skipped: ADR prose keyed by a slug is a
  document, not a ticket.

One case the ticket did not name. A closed parent with an open child cannot
be stored — a finished tree may not hold live work — so the parent is
imported open, because the child says there is work left in it, and it is
reported rather than done quietly. It does not occur in the corpus; it is
covered by a test.

The staleness constraint from 38 is the shape of the command rather than a
check bolted on. It refuses an export whose own export-state.json disagrees
with the file, and refuses one where the interaction log names ids the export
does not contain — 100 of them on this corpus, which is how a quarter of the
tickets would have gone missing silently. --anyway imports anyway and still
says it is behind. Reading Dolt directly was rejected: a dependency on a
database engine is the thing pidly exists not to have.

The part that was more work than the mapping: JSON. There is no jq here and
there will not be, so json_flat is a scanner in awk — a regex splitting on
braces or commas is wrong on the second description in the corpus. It was
verified line for line against python -m json against all 342 records, and
gives identical output under mawk, nawk and gawk.

Two bugs in it are worth naming, because both passed casual reading:

- 0x80 is not a number in POSIX awk. mawk reads it as 0 concatenated with an
  unset variable named x80, so every comparison against one compared against
  the string 0 and every escaped <, > and & in the corpus came out as 000.
  Decimal throughout now. tests/migrate.sh pins it, and was verified to fail
  by putting 0x80 back.
- Unescaping with a pile of gsubs decodes twice. A description holding the
  two characters backslash and n is encoded as three, and any order of gsubs
  turns it into a line break. It is one left-to-right pass now. Two of the
  332 tickets are exactly that case, and the cross-check against python is
  what found both — reading the output would not have.

tests/migrate.sh is 59 assertions on a hand-written corpus, not on the downstream project,
which is not in this repository and will not be. It asserts the corpus still
contains the escapes it exists to exercise, because an escape that got
unescaped on its way into the file would make those tests pass without
testing anything.

Not done: GitHub issues, and anything else emitting JSONL or CSV. The command
takes the source as an argument so adding one does not change its shape.
